Approach 3: Optimal — Monotonic Deque

This is the standard optimal solution.

The key question is:

Do we really need every element in the window?

Suppose:

[1, 3, -1]

We're looking for the maximum.

Since 3 > 1, once 3 enters the window, 1 can never become the maximum while 3 is present.

So 1 is useless.

Similarly, if we have:

[3, -1, 5]

When 5 arrives:

5 > -1
5 > 3

Both -1 and 3 can be removed because 5 is newer and larger.

This gives us the central idea.

Maintain a decreasing deque

The deque stores indices, not values.

The values corresponding to those indices are always in decreasing order.

For example:

nums = [1,3,-1]


Deque values:


[3, -1]

Notice:

3 >= -1

The front always contains the index of the current maximum.

Why Store Indices?

We need two pieces of information:

1. Value

To compare elements:

nums[q.peekLast()] <= nums[i]
2. Index

To determine whether an element has left the window:

q.peek() < i - k + 1

Therefore, we store indices.

The Three Important Operations

For every i, we perform three steps.

Step 1: Remove expired elements

Current window starts at:

i - k + 1

Therefore, any index smaller than this is outside the window.

while (!q.isEmpty() && q.peek() < i - k + 1) {
    q.poll();
}

Example:

i = 4
k = 3


window = [2,3,4]


window start = 4 - 3 + 1
             = 2

So indices:

0, 1

are expired.

Step 2: Remove smaller elements from the back

Suppose the deque represents:

[5, 3, 2]

and the new element is:

6

Since:

6 > 2
6 > 3
6 > 5

all those elements can be removed.

Why?

Because 6:

is larger
is newer
will remain in the window longer

Therefore, none of those smaller elements can ever become the maximum again before 6 leaves.

Code:

while (!q.isEmpty() && nums[q.peekLast()] <= nums[i]) {
    q.pollLast();
}
Step 3: Add current index
q.offer(i);

Now the deque remains monotonically decreasing by value.

Your Code — Optimal Solution

Your code is exactly the standard O(n) monotonic deque solution:

class Solution {
    public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n=nums.length;
        int ans[]=new int[n-k+1];
        Deque<Integer> q=new ArrayDeque<>();
        int ind=0;


        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){


            // Remove indices which are outside the window
            while(!q.isEmpty()&&q.peek()<i-k+1){
                q.poll();
            }


            // Remove smaller elements from the back
            while(!q.isEmpty()&&nums[q.peekLast()]<=nums[i]){
                q.pollLast();
            }


            // Add current index
            q.offer(i);


            // Start storing answers when first window is complete
            if(i>=k-1){
                ans[ind++]=nums[q.peek()];
            }
        }


        return ans;
    }
}
