Why Is the Deque O(n)?

At first glance, we have while loops inside a for loop.

You might think:

O(n²)

But that's not the case.

Every index is:

added to the deque once
removed from the deque at most once

For example:

Index 1 → added → eventually removed
Index 2 → added → eventually removed
Index 3 → added → eventually removed
...

An element cannot be removed repeatedly.

Therefore, across the entire algorithm, the total number of deque operations is at most proportional to 2n.

Hence:

Time = O(n)

And the deque contains at most k elements:

Space = O(k)

Output array requires:

O(n-k+1)

additional space.

Comparison of All Approaches
Approach	Idea	Time	Extra Space
Brute Force	Scan every window	O(nk)	O(1)
Max Heap	Maintain maximum with heap	O(n log k)	O(k)
Monotonic Deque	Maintain decreasing candidates	O(n)	O(k)
The Most Important Intuition

Remember this rule:

If a new element is greater than an element behind it, that smaller element can never be the maximum again.

For example:

Deque:


[8, 6, 4, 2]

New element:

7

Remove:

2
4

because:

7 > 2
7 > 4

But stop at 8:

[8, 6, 7]

Actually, continue from the back:

2 removed
4 removed
6 removed

because 7 > 6.

Final:

[8, 7]

Now 8 is still larger than 7, so the deque remains decreasing.

This is the monotonic decreasing deque.

One Small Detail in Your Code

You used:

while(!q.isEmpty()&&nums[q.peekLast()]<=nums[i]){
    q.pollLast();
}

The <= is intentional.

Suppose:

nums = [5, 5]

When the second 5 arrives, we remove the older 5.

Why?

Because both have the same value, but the newer one has a larger index, so it will stay in the sliding window longer.

Thus:

<=

is generally preferable here.

Interview Explanation

If the interviewer asks "Explain your optimal approach", you can say:

I use a monotonic decreasing deque that stores indices. The front of the deque always contains the index of the maximum element of the current window. For every new element, I first remove indices that are outside the current window. Then I remove elements from the back whose values are smaller than or equal to the current element, because they can never become the maximum while the current element is present. Finally, I add the current index. Once the first window is complete, the value at the front is the maximum. Each index is inserted and removed at most once, giving an overall time complexity of O(n) and auxiliary space of O(k).

